As an I2C master, I am using an [Analog Discovery 3](https://digilent.com/shop/analog-discovery-3/), but of course any other type of logic analyzer or similar should be fine. I set it up to provide 3.3V to the MCU & configure the pins for the I2C bus. Then I simply write some random bytes to the MCU (I2C address 0x01) & verify that they are ACK'ed, as described above. Setting the breakpoint & starting two writes in a row however leads to the second write being NACK'ed as described.
I hope this helps

[KRASTM - 2024-07-22T15:56:31Z]
Hello @lukas-lang,

Thank you for the example. I will try to reproduce the issue using it.
I noticed this Line of code LL_I2C_SetTransferSize(hi2c->Instance, 1); // set NBYTES to 1 to release clock & start receiving

The **TransferSize** is "The number of bytes to be transferred", did you intentionally set it to 1?

